2186284704 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284705 13194139534374 true] [17592186284704 1110 #uuid "aaab0f71-27e7-5c7b-8efe-1d883386aa48" 13194139534374 true] [17592186284705 1034 "<" 13194139534374 true] [17592186284705 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534374 true] [17592186284705 1111 #uuid "6eae45c9-938f-56f9-af1f-676013f1786c" 13194139534374 true] [17592186284706 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284707 13194139534374 true] [17592186284706 1089 "q-87827" 13194139534374 true] [17592186284706 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534374 true] [17592186284706 1100 "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}\\le 4.$" 13194139534374 true] [17592186284706 1113 #uuid "6442f940-7379-5d45-a797-9e3a79792102" 13194139534374 true] [17592186284706 1145 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x} & \\le 4 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x} & \\le \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534374 true] [17592186284707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284708 13194139534374 true] [17592186284707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284709 13194139534374 true] [17592186284707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284710 13194139534374 true] [17592186284707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284711 13194139534374 true] [17592186284707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284712 13194139534374 true] [17592186284707 1040 #edb/ref 17592186